# stateless and stateful

# Stateless: No past data. They do not depend on the previously stored information. Example can be an application

# Stateful: the applications need to maintain the data or identity accross requests, restarts or pod rescheduling. i.e even if a pod crashes or the pods are scaled, the data will reflect in the new pods. example can be database

# Deployment objects are used to deploy stateless apps. StatefulSet obejects are used to deploy stateful app like databases.

# Deployment ---> app pods. when we scale the pods, all the pods will create at once

# StatefulSet ---> database pods. when we scale, the new pods will create by cloning the data from the old pods and hence will deploy one after another.

# manifest file for statefulset
---
apiVersion:v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: ss-name
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: swiggy
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: swiggy
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: cont-1
          image: nginx
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80

# the pods created via stateful set will follow indexing. Deleting pods will follow LIFO

# Ususally volumes are used to replicate data.

< DaemonSet >

# helps to create a pod for every node not to run the apps but to cpature data related to the node to monitor and can do multiple purposes like logging, executing scripts, etc. When a new node is created, a pod will be created and when a node is deleted, the pod will also be deleted.

# manifest file for statefulset
---
apiVersion:v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: monitor
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: swiggy
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: swiggy
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: cont-1
          image: nginx
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80

